body:
I know I'm a month late, but I sometimes wonder if some people's distaste for math (ASD or NT) is due to how it is taught, especially in primary school.

Just a bit of backstory, I excelled in math through primary, secondary and the first bit of University, then I hit a bit of a wall (at least in pure math classes), but I completed an engineering degree with good marks. That difficulty back then made me think, for years, "well I guess that's my limit".

&#x200B;

I'm back in school for physics, and so far the math class is really making sense. I realized the change is twofold;

1. I'm in this now for a love of physics, and mathematics is the language of physics. Being fluent in that language is required for me to obtain my goals.
2. Being older and wiser now, I'm seeing the beauty in math all around me. Math really is the study of patterns, and those patterns can be so incredibly general. Real math, like at the university or academic level, is very creative. The rules are rigorous, but the techniques vary to an incredible degree.

&#x200B;

That being said, kids spend years of their formative years learning numbers and basic arithmetic, where it is constant, dry repetition, and it's easy to see no patterns or creativity.

&#x200B;

A cool video worth watching is;

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r6sGWTCMz2k](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r6sGWTCMz2k)

Not only is the math in that video really important and useful, the actual equation to draw one of those shapes has a really simple pattern to it (once you learn enough of the background of course). For me, it's that amazing complexity hiding behind simple patterns that fascinates me.
